sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch08-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The near-universal 137.5° (golden-ratio) angle between successive plant organs is not an optimization found by natural selection but the inevitable result of a simple process where each new element forms where a chemical concentration is highest and depletes it nearby.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The 137.5° phyllotaxis angle results from a simple inhibition growth process";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 8, “Implications for Everyday Systems”; section “Growth of Plants and Animals” (from p. 400), p. 408. Scope: phyllotaxis and spiral arrangements of plant organs"
}
